# Keep the deploy payload to source only.
#
# Without this the CLI uploaded the local .next directory — about 200 MB of
# build output Vercel neither needs nor uses, since it builds remotely from
# source. On a flaky connection that upload stalls, and a stalled upload is
# worse than a failed one: the CLI dedupes by file hash, so a deployment can
# be created from whichever files did make it, producing a "Ready" build that
# quietly contains stale code.
#
# Source-only payload is well under a megabyte.
#
# .env*.local IS THE IMPORTANT ONE. .vercelignore is the CLI's only ignore
# list — it does not read .gitignore — so a file being safely out of git
# says nothing about whether it is uploaded. Local env files were therefore
# shipped with every deploy, and the moment one of them held a real key the
# deployments stopped building: created, zero build output, status UNKNOWN,
# which is what a credential-scan block looks like from the outside.
#
# Production values belong in `vercel env add`, never in the payload.

.next
node_modules
.vercel/output
.git
scripts
*.tsbuildinfo
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
